WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.120
An earthworm has no bones.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.440
What does it push against to drive through soil?

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.180
The first film showed that a body plan does not reveal ancestry.

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.860
This film asks what a body plan is for.

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.530
Zoom in on one segment of the earthworm.

6
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.050
Its coelom holds fluid.

7
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.770
Thin walls called septa largely separate it from the segments on either side.

8
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:32.193
Two muscle layers wrap the body wall.

9
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:35.953
Circular muscles run around it, like rings.

10
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:39.753
Longitudinal muscles run along the body.

11
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:43.757
Fluid cannot be squeezed into a smaller volume.

12
00:00:45.057 --> 00:00:49.537
So fluid held in by a muscular wall gives muscles something to push against.

13
00:00:50.937 --> 00:00:54.337
This is a hydrostatic skeleton, like water in a balloon.

14
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:57.457
Here we treat one segment as sealed.

15
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.410
The coelom does a second job.

16
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.730
Organs inside it, such as the gut, can grow and move with some independence from the

17
00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:07.730
body wall.

18
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.233
Now the circular muscles contract, and the segment gets narrower.

19
00:01:13.453 --> 00:01:15.233
The fluid has nowhere to go.

20
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:19.253
Does the segment get longer, shorter, or stay the same?

21
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:32.067
It gets longer. The volume must stay the same, so a narrower segment has to stretch.

22
00:01:33.387 --> 00:01:35.787
The septa move apart as the end faces shrink.

23
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:39.347
Now the longitudinal muscles contract.

24
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.267
The segment gets shorter, so at the same volume it must get fatter.

25
00:01:45.687 --> 00:01:48.467
Tiny bristles called setae poke out and grip the soil.

26
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.560
Because septa partition the coelom, neighbours can take different shapes.

27
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.320
In textbook accounts, each segment works largely on its own.

28
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.660
One is long and thin, reaching forward.

29
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.160
Its neighbour is short, fat and anchored.

30
00:02:06.233 --> 00:02:08.653
Pass these shapes down the body as a wave.

31
00:02:09.913 --> 00:02:11.733
Long, thin segments reach ahead.

32
00:02:13.113 --> 00:02:15.633
Short, fat segments hold the ground behind them.

33
00:02:16.773 --> 00:02:18.613
And the worm moves forward through the soil.

34
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:22.167
The same body plan has a second payoff.

35
00:02:23.447 --> 00:02:25.627
The gut runs through, from mouth to anus.

36
00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:31.347
Food moves one way, so successive regions can specialize in different jobs.

37
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.810
A muscular pharynx takes food in.

38
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:39.430
A crop stores it. A gizzard grinds it.

39
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.910
A long intestine digests and absorbs it.

40
00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:45.803
So the earthworm needs no bones.

41
00:02:47.243 --> 00:02:49.163
Fluid held in by muscle is its skeleton.

42
00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.983
The volume stays the same.

43
00:02:53.403 --> 00:02:55.103
Narrow a segment, and it lengthens.

44
00:02:56.563 --> 00:02:57.563
Shorten it, and it widens.
